[section:gauss_jacobi Gauss-Jacobi quadrature]

[heading Synopsis]

`#include <boost/math/quadrature/gauss_jacobi.hpp>`

   namespace boost{ namespace math{ namespace quadrature{

   template <class Real, class ``__Policy`` = boost::math::policies::policy<> >
   class gauss_jacobi
   {
   public:
      gauss_jacobi(unsigned N, Real alpha, Real beta);

      const std::vector<Real>& abscissa() const;
      const std::vector<Real>& weights() const;
      bool anti_gauss_is_internal() const;
      const std::vector<Real>& anti_gauss_abscissa() const;
      const std::vector<Real>& anti_gauss_weights() const;

      template <class F>
      auto integrate(F f, Real* pL1 = nullptr) const -> decltype(std::declval<F>()(std::declval<Real>()));

      template <class F, class Norm>
      auto integrate(F f, const decltype(f(Real(0)))& zero, Norm norm, Real* pL1 = nullptr) const -> decltype(f(Real(0)));

      template <class F>
      auto integrate_averaged(F f, Real* error = nullptr, Real* pL1 = nullptr) const -> decltype(std::declval<F>()(std::declval<Real>()));

      template <class F, class Norm>
      auto integrate_averaged(F f, const decltype(f(Real(0)))& zero, Norm norm,
                              Real* error = nullptr, Real* pL1 = nullptr) const -> decltype(f(Real(0)));
   };

   }}} // namespaces

[heading Description]

The `gauss_jacobi` class template computes

[expression ['[int][sub -1][super 1] f(x) (1 - x)[super [alpha]] (1 + x)[super [beta]] dx [approx] [sum][sub i] w[sub i] f(x[sub i])]]

using /N/ evaluations of /f/, where the abscissas /x/[sub i] are the zeros of the Jacobi polynomial
/P/[sub /N/][super ([alpha], [beta])], which [link math_toolkit.sf_poly.jacobi `jacobi_zeros`] also returns.
The rule is exact when /f/ is a polynomial of degree at most 2['N] - 1.
Note that the weight function (1 - /x/)[super [alpha]] (1 + /x/)[super [beta]] is not part of /f/: pass only the factor that multiplies it.
This is what makes the rule effective for integrands with algebraic singularities at the ends of the interval;
[alpha] = [beta] = 0 gives Gauss-Legendre quadrature, [alpha] = [beta] = -1/2 Gauss-Chebyshev quadrature,
and other intervals \[/a/, /b/\] follow from the change of variables /t/ = ((/b/ - /a/)/x/ + /a/ + /b/)/2.

Like [link math_toolkit.gauss `gauss`], this is a "one shot" non-adaptive routine, intended for smooth /f/.
Unlike `gauss`, the number of points is a run-time argument, and the rule is computed by the constructor.
For /N/ + 1 more evaluations of /f/, `integrate_averaged` is exact to higher degree and estimates its error.

   gauss_jacobi(unsigned N, Real alpha, Real beta);

Constructs the /N/-point rule. /N/ must be positive, and [alpha] and [beta] finite and greater than -1:
otherwise the constructor raises a __domain_error through the /Policy/.
If the policy does not throw, the abscissas and weights are NaN, and so is every integral.

   const std::vector<Real>& abscissa() const;
   const std::vector<Real>& weights() const;

These provide direct access to the abscissas, all in (-1, 1) and in increasing order, and their weights.

   bool anti_gauss_is_internal() const;
   const std::vector<Real>& anti_gauss_abscissa() const;
   const std::vector<Real>& anti_gauss_weights() const;

These describe the anti-Gauss rule /A/ of `integrate_averaged`, below.
Its nodes lie in \[-1, 1\] unless [alpha] or [beta] is less than -1/2 and /N/ is large, or near -1/2 for some /N/ (Laurie, Theorem 3),
and `anti_gauss_is_internal` tells which: when it returns `false` there is no averaged rule, and the two vectors are empty.
For [alpha] or [beta] equal to -1/2 the anti-Gauss rule can have nodes on -1 and 1, where /f/ is then evaluated.
The rule is computed the first time it is needed, then cached.

   template <class F>
   auto integrate(F f, Real* pL1 = nullptr) const -> decltype(std::declval<F>()(std::declval<Real>()));

Integrates /f/(/x/) (1 - /x/)[super [alpha]] (1 + /x/)[super [beta]] over \[-1, 1\] with the /N/-point Gauss rule /G/. /f/ may return a real or complex value.

If `pL1` is not null, `*pL1` is set to the corresponding integral of |/f/|.
If that is substantially larger than the absolute value of the result, then the sum was ill-conditioned.

   template <class F, class Norm>
   auto integrate(F f, const decltype(f(Real(0)))& zero, Norm norm, Real* pL1 = nullptr) const -> decltype(f(Real(0)));

As for [link math_toolkit.gauss `gauss`], this overload handles vector- and matrix-valued integrands:
`zero` is the additive identity of the returned type, and `norm` returns a nonnegative scalar convertible to `Real`.
The L1 norm is measured with `norm`.

   template <class F>
   auto integrate_averaged(F f, Real* error = nullptr, Real* pL1 = nullptr) const -> decltype(std::declval<F>()(std::declval<Real>()));

   template <class F, class Norm>
   auto integrate_averaged(F f, const decltype(f(Real(0)))& zero, Norm norm,
                           Real* error = nullptr, Real* pL1 = nullptr) const -> decltype(f(Real(0)));

Returns Laurie's averaged rule (/G/(/f/) + /A/(/f/))/2, at the cost of /N/ + 1 more evaluations of /f/.
/A/ is the anti-Gauss rule: the rule with /N/ + 1 nodes whose error on every polynomial of degree up to 2['N] + 1
is exactly that of /G/ with the opposite sign, so the average is exact for polynomials of degree 2['N] + 1.
Its nodes interlace the Gauss nodes and its weights are positive.
If `anti_gauss_is_internal()` is `false`, these functions raise an __evaluation_error through the /Policy/;
if the policy does not throw, the result, `*error` and `*pL1` are NaN.

If `error` is not null, `*error` is set to |/A/(/f/) - /G/(/f/)|/2, but never less than 2[epsilon] times the L1 norm.
When /G/ and /A/ bracket the integral, as they do for smooth /f/ once the error falls steadily with /N/, this bounds the error of the average;
it is then also close to the error of /G/.
Otherwise it is a heuristic, and integrands that alias onto the null space of /A/ - /G/ can fool it.
If `pL1` is not null, `*pL1` is set to the L1 norm of the averaged rule, measured with `norm` in the second overload.

[heading Example]

With [alpha] = [beta] = -1/2,

[expression ['[int][sub -1][super 1] e[super x] / [radic](1 - x[super 2]) dx = [pi] I[sub 0](1)]]

is computed by

   using boost::math::quadrature::gauss_jacobi;
   gauss_jacobi<double> rule(10, -0.5, -0.5);
   double error;
   double I = rule.integrate_averaged([](double x) { return std::exp(x); }, &error);

`I` is the exact value rounded to double precision, and `error` is 1.8e-15, its floor of 2[epsilon] times the L1 norm.

[heading Computing the rule]

The abscissas and weights are found in O(/N/) operations by following the differential equation of the Jacobi functions
from zero to zero in twice the working precision (Glaser, Liu and Rokhlin), and from the series of the polynomials about the end points near those.
Where that method cannot vouch for a zero, the rule comes from the three-term recurrence instead, which overflows for large /N/ combined with
large [alpha] or [beta] (in `double`, for example /N/ = 20000 and [alpha] = [beta] = 200).
If the rule cannot be computed, `integrate` and `integrate_averaged` raise an __evaluation_error through the /Policy/;
if the policy does not throw, the result is a NaN.
Construct the rule once and reuse it.

[heading References]

* G. H. Golub and J. H. Welsch, ['Calculation of Gauss quadrature rules], Mathematics of Computation 23 (1969), 221-230.
* A. Glaser, X. Liu and V. Rokhlin, ['A fast algorithm for the calculation of the roots of special functions], SIAM Journal on Scientific Computing 29 (2007), no. 4, 1420-1438, [@https://doi.org/10.1137/06067016X doi:10.1137/06067016X].
* W. Gautschi, ['Orthogonal Polynomials: Computation and Approximation], Oxford University Press, 2004.
* D. P. Laurie, ['Anti-Gaussian quadrature formulas], Mathematics of Computation 65 (1996), no. 214, 739-747, [@https://doi.org/10.1090/S0025-5718-96-00713-2 doi:10.1090/S0025-5718-96-00713-2].

[endsect] [/section:gauss_jacobi Gauss-Jacobi quadrature]
